## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-account trust, least privilege S3/KMS usage, no Internet constraint, and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account role trust
- S3 bucket policy with path-level scoping
- KMS policy for decrypt/encrypt
- No Internet access
- Immutable auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Trust policy excerpt for DataAccessRole
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/ProcessorRole"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
```
```javascript
// Bucket policy excerpt (data-inbound)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/DataAccessRole"},
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-inbound",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-inbound/data-inbound/*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/DataAccessRole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-out/*"
    }
  ]
}
```
```javascript
// KMS key policy snippet (CMK in Security account)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/DataAccessRole"},
      "Action": ["kms:Decrypt","kms:GenerateDataKey*","kms:Encrypt"],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for privilege creep?
- How would you rotate the CMK without downtime?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of time-bound access, cross-account trust, and auditable controls for external data sharing in a single AWS account context.

## Key Concepts

- STS AssumeRole with short-lived session
- IAM trust policies and cross-account access
- S3 Access Points and bucket policies for granular prefix access
- TLS enforcement, IP allow-lists, PrivateLink/VPCE
- KMS encryption and CloudTrail/S3 access logs

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::PARTNER_ACCOUNT_ID:root"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "true"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::your-bucket/your-prefix/*"],
      "Condition": {"IpAddress": {"aws:SourceIp": "203.0.113.0/24"}, "Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test and verify revocation works automatically after 14 days?
- What logging and alerting would you add to detect misuse or anomalies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests an advanced multi-account security pattern: tenant isolation, key management, and rapid containment in a live streaming data pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account IAM roles with MFA
- Per-tenant CMKs and rotation policies
- Explicit trust conditions (aws:SourceAccount, aws:SourceArn, aws:RequestedRegion)
- PrivateLink/VPCE for data-plane isolation
- Immutable audit trails via CloudTrail across regions
- Breach-testing and automated containment

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-${aws:userid}-data/*",
        "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-${aws:userid}-data"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:SourceAccount": "${aws:SourceAccount}",
          "aws:RequestedRegion": ["us-east-1", "us-west-2"]
        },
        "ArnEquals": {
          "aws:SourceArn": "arn:aws:iam::${aws:SourceAccount}:role/tenant-analytics-role"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Why This Is Asked
- Tests a practical, scalable multi-tenant pattern within a single bucket, emphasizing isolation, encryption, and rapid containment.
- Exercises policy design using S3, KMS, and IAM with MFA, plus a real-time breach response workflow.

Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation via per-tenant S3 prefix and IAM role scope
- SSE-KMS with per-tenant CMKs and encryption context validation
- Containment orchestration using EventBridge, Lambda, and CloudTrail

Code Example
```json
// S3 bucket policy (illustrative)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/Tenant123Role"},
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-bucket/tenant-123/*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"s3:prefix": "tenant-123/"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// KMS key policy (illustrative)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/Tenant123Role"},
      "Action": ["kms:Encrypt","kms:Decrypt"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"kms:EncryptionContext:TenantID": "tenant-123"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the 60-second containment workflow end-to-end? 
- How would you extend this to support new tenants without increasing policy complexity?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of mutual TLS in AWS, certificate lifecycle, and zero-trust patterns for multi-tenant serverless apps. It also probes how rotation, revocation, and test planning are integrated into day-1 security work.

## Key Concepts
- mutual TLS with API Gateway and ACM Private CA
- Lambda authorizer to extract tenant from client certificate and enforce least privilege
- Per-tenant IAM policies and fine-grained access controls
- Certificate revocation lists (CRL) stored centrally (S3 or DynamoDB) and checked at request time
- Certificate/CA rotation strategy and disruption-free rollout
- Test plan including revocation, logging, and incident response

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lambda authorizer skeleton: extract tenant from cert CN and build a policy
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const cert = event.requestContext?.identity?.callerCert;
  const subjectCN = cert?.subject?.CN;
  const tenant = extractTenantFromCN(subjectCN); // custom parsing
  const policy = buildPolicy(tenant, event.methodArn);
  return policy;
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you revoke a single tenant cert and verify cross-resource denial without impacting other tenants?
- What are the trade-offs of using Lambda authorizers vs API Gateway JWT authorizers in this setup?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical cross-account data sharing with fine-grained data protection, lineage, and incident readiness.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account IAM roles and least privilege
- Field-level masking, KMS, Lake Formation
- PII discovery with Macie, immutable logs with CloudTrail/S3 Object Lock

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example pseudo: mask sensitive fields in records
function maskFields(record, fields) {
  const out = {...record};
  for (const f of fields) if (f in out) out[f] = '***';
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the masking policy against evolving data models?
- How would you invalidate stale tokens during a breach simulation?
